#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# Tiny wrapper around target-offset-hook.py's --radec/--polar setup step,
# meant to be the one command a student actually types at the telescope
# in the middle of the night:
#
#   center M31
#   center 00:42:44.3 +41:16:09
#   center 10.6847 41.2688
#   center --polar
#
# Written for a manual-control student telescope project: there is no
# slew command here and nothing here moves the mount - a human is always
# the one turning the controls, watching the offset target-offset-hook.py
# draws in ds9 shrink. This wrapper only resolves what "RA Dec" means (a
# name via Simbad/Sesame, sexagesimal, or plain decimal degrees) and
# writes it to the same config file target-offset-hook.py already reads -
# it does not touch rts2-focusc at all.
#
# Expected nightly workflow: start rts2-focusc ONCE, at the beginning of
# the session, running continuously in its own terminal/screen:
#
#   rts2-focusc -d C0 -e 5 -F /path/to/target-offset-hook.py
#
# Then, in another terminal, re-point as often as needed during the
# night just by running this wrapper - no restart of rts2-focusc needed,
# since target-offset-hook.py re-reads its config fresh on every
# completed exposure, so a new target takes effect within one exposure
# cycle:
#
#   center M31
#   ... watch ds9, turn the mount, offset shrinks ...
#   center Polaris --polar   # or just: center --polar
#
# This is a starting point / sketch, not a shipped/installed tool - not
# built by CMakeLists.txt, not packaged, not covered by any test. Same
# directory as target-offset-hook.py/ds9-hook.sh, which it wraps.
#
